---
// Matched to the real data flows: the contact-form Worker (ADR 0003, spec
// #28) and the Client Portal (spec #29). If either changes what it collects,
// keeps or shares, this page must change with it (FTC Act §5, G.S. 75-1.1).
import LegalLayout from "../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, LEGAL_NAME, MAILING_ADDRESS } from "../site";
---

<LegalLayout title="Privacy Policy" description="How Akimbo Technology handles personal information.">
  <p>
    This policy explains what personal information {LEGAL_NAME} (“Akimbo”, “we”, “us”) collects,
    why, who handles it for us and how long we keep it. It covers this website,
    <code>akimbo.technology</code>, and our Client Portal at <code>portal.akimbo.technology</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    {LEGAL_NAME} is a North Carolina limited liability company that provides managed network
    and infrastructure services to businesses. You can reach us at
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> or by post at {LEGAL_NAME},
    {MAILING_ADDRESS.join(", ")}.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>

  <h3>When you use our contact form</h3>
  <p>
    The form asks for your name, email address, business name and town, and, depending on what
    you’re asking about, your phone number, the rough number of devices you have, the service
    you’re interested in, when suits you, and your message.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you send it, our form handler (a small program that runs on Cloudflare) checks it and
    sends its contents as one email, through Postmark, to our <code>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</code>
    mailbox. The form handler stores nothing. Its logs record only whether the message was sent
    and Postmark’s reference number for it, never what you typed. We send no automatic reply: the
    only email you’ll get is a reply from a person.
  </p>

  <h3>When you email us</h3>
  <p>
    Emails you send us, and our replies, are kept in our Microsoft 365 mailboxes, along with any
    documents we exchange, such as quotes, agreements and network audit reports.
  </p>

  <h3>When you use the Client Portal</h3>
  <p>
    The Client Portal is for people our clients authorise. For each portal user we keep their
    name, email address, which client they belong to, and when they accepted the Client Portal
    Terms and which version. Portal users sign in with a one-time code sent by email, so there is
    no password to store. If a portal user adds a passkey, we store the public part of it; the
    private part never leaves their device.
  </p>
  <p>
    For security we keep a log of sign-ins (successful and failed), sign-in code requests and
    changes to a portal user’s access, with the time and the IP address they came from.
  </p>

  <h3>Technical information</h3>
  <p>
    Cloudflare delivers and protects this site and the Client Portal. To do that it processes
    every request, including your IP address, browser details, the page you asked for and the
    time. It uses that information to deliver pages, block attacks and limit abuse.
  </p>
  <p>
    The contact page, and only that page, loads Cloudflare Turnstile, which checks that a form is
    being sent by a person and not a bot. It does this by looking at information about your
    browser and device, without asking you to solve a puzzle.
  </p>
  <p>
    We don’t use analytics on the site today. If we add them, we’ll use a tool that sets no
    cookies and doesn’t track you across sites, and we’ll update this policy first.
  </p>

  <h3>Our clients’ infrastructure records</h3>
  <p>
    When we audit or manage a client’s network, we record details of their infrastructure, such
    as devices, addresses and circuits, and their staff contacts. The Client Portal shows those
    records to that client’s portal users. We hold this information on our client’s behalf, and
    our signed agreement with that client governs it.
  </p>

  <h2>How we use it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>to answer your enquiry and arrange a network audit or a plan;</li>
    <li>to provide the services in our signed agreements;</li>
    <li>to run the Client Portal, sign portal users in and keep it secure;</li>
    <li>to send service emails, such as sign-in codes;</li>
    <li>to protect our site, our systems and our clients from abuse and attack;</li>
    <li>to keep business records and meet our legal obligations.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We don’t send marketing email. If we ever start, we’ll ask first or give you a simple way to
    opt out of every message.
  </p>

  <h2>Who we share it with</h2>
  <p>
    We do not sell personal information. We do not share it for targeted advertising, and we
    don’t use advertising trackers. We share it only with the service providers who run parts of
    our business for us, and only so they can do that work:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare</strong>: hosts this site and our contact-form handler, provides DNS,
      security and Turnstile, and carries traffic to the Client Portal. It keeps request logs.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Postmark</strong>: delivers the emails our contact form and the Client Portal send.
      Postmark’s open and click tracking is turned off for all of our email.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Microsoft 365</strong>: our email, shared mailbox and documents.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Our own server</strong>: the Client Portal and our clients’ infrastructure records
      run on a server we own and operate ourselves, not on a third-party host.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Backblaze</strong>: stores encrypted backups of that server.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We may also share information with our professional advisers, such as lawyers and
    accountants; when the law requires it; to protect our rights or someone’s safety; or with a
    buyer if our business is sold, who would have to keep to this policy.
  </p>

  <h2>Cookies</h2>
  <p>
    We use only strictly necessary cookies, so there is no cookie banner.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      This site sets no cookies of its own. Cloudflare may set strictly necessary cookies, such as
      <code>__cf_bm</code> and <code>cf_clearance</code>, to tell people from bots, and Turnstile
      may do the same on the contact page.
    </li>
    <li>
      The Client Portal sets a session cookie that keeps you signed in and a security cookie that
      protects its forms. A session ends after 1 hour without use or 8 hours in total.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>We don’t use analytics, advertising or social-media cookies.</p>

  <h2>Do Not Track</h2>
  <p>
    We don’t track visitors across other websites, and we don’t let third parties do so on ours.
    Because there is no such tracking to switch off, our site works the same way whether or not
    your browser sends a Do Not Track signal.
  </p>

  <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Enquiries and email:</strong> as long as we need them to answer you and keep our
      business records. Ask us and we’ll delete an enquiry that didn’t lead to any work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Network audits:</strong> if you buy a network audit and don’t go on to a plan, we
      delete our records of your infrastructure 90 days after we walk you through the report. The
      report itself is yours to keep.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Clients who leave:</strong> when a plan ends, portal access is cut off at the
      handover, and we delete our records of the client’s infrastructure 30 days after the
      handover.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Client Portal security log:</strong> we keep the record of who had access, and when,
      for as long as we need it to answer security and access questions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Backups:</strong> deleted information ages out of our encrypted backups within 12
      months.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Billing records:</strong> as long as tax and accounting law requires.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How we protect it</h2>
  <p>
    We use reasonable safeguards for a business of our size. They include encryption in transit,
    encrypted backups, multi-factor sign-in for our own accounts, and keeping each client’s
    records separate so one client can’t see another’s. No system is perfectly secure, so we
    can’t promise that information will never be lost or exposed. If a breach affects your
    information, we’ll tell you as the law requires.
  </p>

  <h2>Your choices</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      To see, correct or delete personal information we hold about you, email
      <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. We may need to check who you are
      first, and we may keep information that the law or a signed agreement requires us to keep.
    </li>
    <li>
      A client’s portal users are added and removed at the written request of that client’s
      primary contact. If you no longer need access, ask them or us.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    Our services are for businesses. The site isn’t aimed at children under 13, and we don’t
    knowingly collect their information. If you think a child has sent us information, contact
    us and we’ll delete it.
  </p>

  <h2>Other websites</h2>
  <p>
    The site may link to websites we don’t control. Their own privacy policies apply there.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    When we change this policy we’ll post the new version here and update the effective date at
    the top of this page. If a change is significant, we’ll also tell portal users by email
    before it takes effect.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact us</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about this policy or your information can go to
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> or by post to {LEGAL_NAME},
    {MAILING_ADDRESS.join(", ")}.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
